stream
BT /F2 8 Tf 54.00 814.00 Td (Comprehensive Interview Q&A) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 536.60 24.00 Td (3) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 772.00 Td (22. Why not power all rails at the same time?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 748.00 Td (Some ICs require a specific rail order. If I/O powers before the core, current can flow) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 734.00 Td (through protection structures and cause undefined behavior.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 711.00 Td (23. What is back-powering?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 687.00 Td (Back-powering happens when an unpowered rail or chip is unintentionally powered through) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 673.00 Td (another signal path, often through I/O protection diodes.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 650.00 Td (24. What is latch-up?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 626.00 Td (Latch-up is a destructive condition where parasitic internal structures in an IC conduct) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 612.00 Td (heavily. It can happen due to invalid voltage conditions or injected current.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 589.00 Td (25. How can bad sequencing cause latch-up?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 565.00 Td (If I/O pins are powered while the core is off, internal structures may be biased) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 551.00 Td (incorrectly, allowing current paths that should not exist.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 528.00 Td (26. Why is Rail C turned on last?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 504.00 Td (Rail C powers sensors/peripherals. These loads should start after the core and I/O are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 490.00 Td (ready, and they may draw significant transient current.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 467.00 Td (27. What is a load transient?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 443.00 Td (A load transient is a sudden change in current demand.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 420.00 Td (28. Why simulate a Rail C load transient?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 396.00 Td (Sensors or cameras may suddenly wake up and draw much more current. The simulation) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 382.00 Td (checks whether the 3.3 V rail droops too much.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 359.00 Td (29. What is the Rail C load step?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 335.00 Td (Rail C is modeled as stepping from a light load to a heavier active load around 8 ms.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 312.00 Td (30. How did you calculate load resistance?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 288.00 Td (I used Ohm's law: `R = V / I`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 265.00 Td (31. What is the Rail A load resistance?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 241.00 Td (For 1.2 V at 0.5 A, `R = 1.2 / 0.5 = 2.4 ohm`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 218.00 Td (32. What is the Rail B load resistance?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 194.00 Td (For 1.8 V at 0.6 A, `R = 1.8 / 0.6 = 3.0 ohm`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 171.00 Td (33. What is the Rail C full-load resistance?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 147.00 Td (For 3.3 V at 0.8 A, `R = 3.3 / 0.8 = 4.125 ohm`.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 54.00 124.00 Td (34. Why is Rail C modeled with a current source?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 100.00 Td (A current source is better for modeling a dynamic load like a sensor waking up because) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54.00 86.00 Td (the current demand changes with time.) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
